WHAT: with WK_KCALC_SELFTEST=1, narrate the result display (and the number of
      visible top-level widgets) to stderr on every change.
WHY:  so a headless harness can assert on something other than pixels.
      "It links" is worth very little for this port, and a pixel histogram
      proves only "something was painted" -- it cannot distinguish 42 from 43
      without OCR. The claim worth making is that a real key event reached
      KCalculator, went through KCalc own expression parser, was evaluated by
      KNumber -- i.e. by GMP, MPFR and MPC, three autotools cross-builds that
      nothing else in this port exercises -- and came back as the right number.
      That is a STRING, so the app prints the string.
      BOTH displays are narrated, because they are different widgets answering
      different questions and only one of them is where typing lands:
        input    KCalcInputDisplay, a QLineEdit -- the expression being typed
        display  KCalcDisplay -- the RESULT, empty until the expression parses
      A harness watching only `display` sees nothing at all while keys arrive
      perfectly, which reads exactly like keys not arriving. (It did, on the
      first run of the harness.)
      `tops` is the count of visible top-level widgets: 1 is the KXmlGuiWindow,
      more means a dialog is open. It is the cheapest way for a harness to tell
      a modal settings dialog from the main window.
      Guarded on __wasi__ AND on the environment variable, so a desktop build
      of this tree is byte-for-byte unaffected and a wk node without the
      variable prints nothing.
UPSTREAM: no. This is ours, for testing, and not upstream business.
diff --git a/kcalc.cpp b/kcalc.cpp
index e0ff333..b3b0223 100644
--- a/kcalc.cpp
+++ b/kcalc.cpp
@@ -57,6 +57,62 @@ const int maxprecision = 1000;
 // Name: KCalculator
 // Desc: constructor
 //------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// --- wk selftest -----------------------------------------------------------
+// With WK_KCALC_SELFTEST=1 in the node's environment, narrate what the
+// calculator is SHOWING to stderr, which a wk node's log carries.
+//
+// WHY NARRATION AND NOT PIXELS. A harness can prove "something was painted"
+// from a histogram, and that is worth very little here: the interesting claim
+// is that a keystroke reached KCalculator through Qt's shortcut machinery,
+// went through KCalc's own expression parser, was evaluated by KNumber -- i.e.
+// by GMP, MPFR and MPC, three autotools cross-builds nothing else in this port
+// exercises -- and came back as a number. No arrangement of pixels
+// distinguishes 42 from 43 without OCR. So the app says what it is showing and
+// the harness asserts on the string.
+//
+// BOTH displays, because they are different widgets answering different
+// questions and only one of them is where typing lands:
+//   input   KCalcInputDisplay, a QLineEdit -- the expression being typed;
+//   display KCalcDisplay -- the RESULT, which stays empty until `=`.
+// A harness watching only `display` sees nothing at all while keys arrive
+// perfectly, which reads exactly like keys not arriving.
+// tops is the count of visible top-level widgets: 1 is the KXmlGuiWindow,
+// more means a dialog is open.
+//
+// Guarded on __wasi__ AND on the environment variable, so a desktop build of
+// this tree is byte-for-byte unaffected.
+#ifdef __wasi__
+#include <cstdio>
+static void wkSelftestInstall(QWidget *win, QLineEdit *input, KCalcDisplay *display)
+{
+    if (!qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("WK_KCALC_SELFTEST")) {
+        return;
+    }
+    auto say = [input, display]() {
+        int tops = 0;
+        const auto widgets = QApplication::topLevelWidgets();
+        for (const QWidget *w : widgets) {
+            if (w->isVisible()) {
+                ++tops;
+            }
+        }
+        std::fprintf(stderr,
+                     "STATE input='%s' display='%s' tops=%d\n",
+                     qUtf8Printable(input->text()),
+                     qUtf8Printable(display->text()),
+                     tops);
+        std::fflush(stderr);
+    };
+    QObject::connect(input, &QLineEdit::textChanged, win, [say](const QString &) {
+        say();
+    });
+    QObject::connect(display, &KCalcDisplay::changedText, win, [say](const QString &) {
+        say();
+    });
+    say();
+}
+#endif
+
 KCalculator::KCalculator(QWidget *parent)
     : KXmlGuiWindow(parent)
     , m_memoryNum(0.0)
@@ -151,6 +207,10 @@ KCalculator::KCalculator(QWidget *parent)
     m_isStillInLaunch = false;
 
     calc_display->setFocus();
+
+#ifdef __wasi__
+    wkSelftestInstall(this, input_display, calc_display);
+#endif
 }
 
